WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.590
Our best team topped the course

2
00:00:02.590 --> 00:00:04.490
leaderboard by a wide margin.

3
00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.480
Then we noticed they had tuned

4
00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:09.220
forty models on the test set.

5
00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:12.190
Their final score measured their

6
00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:14.190
persistence, not their model.

7
00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:17.840
Today: how models are evaluated

8
00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:21.100
honestly, how to document them, and how

9
00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:23.060
to assess machine learning students

10
00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:24.960
when they can use language models.

11
00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:29.000
Honest evaluation uses three splits.

12
00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:32.250
Train fits the weights, validation

13
00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:34.910
chooses the model and settings, and

14
00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:37.230
test is opened once, at the end.

15
00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:40.070
Every peek at test makes its

16
00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:41.690
score more optimistic.

17
00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:45.680
With one percent positives, a model

18
00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.480
that always says no is ninety nine

19
00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:50.520
percent accurate and useless.

20
00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:54.490
Report precision, recall and calibration,

21
00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:57.010
and break results down by subgroup.

22
00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:00.320
This classifier has ninety one

23
00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:01.580
percent recall overall.

24
00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:05.340
Split by group, it is ninety four percent

25
00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:07.920
for one and seventy two for the other.

26
00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:10.630
The average hid the gap.

27
00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:13.670
Mitchell and colleagues proposed

28
00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:16.430
model cards: a short document with

29
00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:19.590
intended use, training data, metrics

30
00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:21.850
by subgroup, and known limits.

31
00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:23.970
Asking students to write one

32
00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:25.710
makes evaluation a habit.

33
00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:28.360
Students can generate

34
00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:30.120
working code in seconds.

35
00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:34.000
So assess what a model cannot do for them.

36
00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:37.030
Use oral defences of their own

37
00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:40.030
notebooks, require an error analysis on

38
00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:42.510
their data, and grade the process.

39
00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:44.290
Quick check!

40
00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:47.100
A team picked the best of forty

41
00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:50.000
models by validation score and

42
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:51.760
reported that same score.

43
00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:55.160
Is it an honest estimate of

44
00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:56.440
performance on new data?

45
00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:57.990
No.

46
00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:00.560
Picking the maximum of forty noisy

47
00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:02.980
scores selects luck as well as skill.

48
00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:05.600
Any score used to choose is

49
00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.560
optimistic, so report a fresh test score.

50
00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:12.240
In the app, open the overfitting lab.

51
00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:15.460
Raise Freedom step by step and

52
00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:17.560
watch the error on new examples,

53
00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:19.880
which plays the validation set.

54
00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:22.910
Then ask students where they would stop,

55
00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:26.210
and why the test set must stay closed.

56
00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:28.290
Your turn.

57
00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:31.930
Fraud is one transaction in a thousand.

58
00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:35.340
A model flags nothing and scores ninety

59
00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:37.440
nine point nine percent accuracy.

60
00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:39.870
Which metric exposes it?

61
00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:43.090
Recall, which is zero.

62
00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:45.940
Remember: open the test set once,

63
00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:49.120
report metrics by subgroup, write a

64
00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:51.400
model card, and assess what language

65
00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:53.240
models cannot do for students.

66
00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:56.990
Try it this term: add a ten minute

67
00:02:56.990 --> 00:02:58.870
oral defence to your next project.

68
00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:01.720
Ask each student to explain one

69
00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:04.020
error their model makes and one

70
00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:05.480
design choice they would change.
